  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nHigh temperatures spiked across the area this afternoon ahead of\na cold front that is forecast to cross the area tonight. This\nfront should eventually act as a trigger for some high based\nshower and thunderstorm development, from roughly the Tri- \nCities east this evening. While severe weather is not expected,\nplenty of elevated instability along with an inverted V \nstructure in soundings indicates there could be some stronger \nwind gusts with the initial storm development. Once the front\ntracks east by late evening, a second round of showers and\nthunderstorm moving in from the west could again impact\nportions of mainly south central Nebraska overnight.\n\nBehind the cold front, expect a noticably cooler afternoon\nacross the area with high temperatures expected to fall 15 to 20\ndegrees from this afternoons high temperatures to start the\nweekend. While most of the area will remain dry during the\ndaytime hours, models advertise another round of showers and\nthunderstorms impacting parts of the area overnight Saturday\nnight as an upper level low approaches from the west. This low\nis then expected to cross the local area Sunday, with a\nreinforcing shot of cooler air along with cloudy skies likely\nkeeping high temperatures in the lower to middle 60s for most\nSunday afternoon.\n\nThereafter...a seasonably cool week (normal highs are in the mid\nto upper 70s) along with multiple (mainly small) chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms are anticipated as west southwesterly\nflow aloft continues with multiple embedded disturbances\nexpected through at least the end of next week.",
